// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6438 Add Linux rule: Potential Shell Or Command Execution Via Xargs
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6438
// Pull request author: defamp
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Shell Or Command Execution Via Xargs
// Sigma ID: b9d97509-9e2f-4086-810a-f289b78aec3b
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Defa Mulya Pratama
// Date: 2026-10-07 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1059
// Description: Detects the use of "xargs" with the "-a /dev/null" (or "--arg-file=/dev/null") option to execute
//   a command with no input. This is a GTFOBins technique used to spawn a shell or run arbitrary
//   commands, and when "xargs" is executed with elevated privileges (e.g. via sudo) it is abused for
//   privilege escalation or to break out of restricted environments.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely. Reading arguments from "/dev/null" provides no input and is rarely used in legitimate automation.
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/xargs/
//   - https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/security/current/linux-restricted-shell-breakout-via-linux-binary-s.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/61e8a9a31c2f90fc7eff8601c2e3dda08bb09d52/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_xargs_shell_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/xargs" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "-a /dev/null" or ProcessCommandLine contains "--arg-file=/dev/null" or ProcessCommandLine contains "--arg-file /dev/null")
